1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,456
That's the question the chapter opens with.
A CODEOWNERS file maps path patterns to

2
00:00:09,496 --> 00:00:13,127
the users and teams whom GitHub
asks to review changes there.

3
00:00:13,167 --> 00:00:17,280
The textbook gives six ordinary
answers. No rule required the review.

4
00:00:17,320 --> 00:00:20,771
A later line in the file took
the path away from the team.

5
00:00:20,811 --> 00:00:24,563
The team has no explicit write
access, so its line was skipped.

6
00:00:24,603 --> 00:00:29,559
The pull request was a draft. The file that
counted was the one on the base branch.

7
00:00:29,599 --> 00:00:32,388
Or the person who merged
could bypass the rule.

8
00:00:32,728 --> 00:00:36,133
Each of the six is documented
behavior. None is a bug.

9
00:00:36,173 --> 00:00:41,173
After this video you can explain the first
three, and the next video covers the

10
00:00:41,213 --> 00:00:45,320
rest. Keep the second one in mind,
the later line. It comes back.

11
00:00:47,160 --> 00:00:50,741
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:50,781 --> 00:00:55,549
You already know the gitignore pattern
language from the video on ignoring files:

13
00:00:55,589 --> 00:00:59,111
patterns that tell Git which
untracked files to leave alone.

14
00:00:59,151 --> 00:01:03,800
And from the last video you know how a
ruleset, a named list of rules, blocks a

15
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:08,549
merge. CODEOWNERS sits between the two.
It borrows most of the gitignore pattern

16
00:01:08,589 --> 00:01:11,695
language, and it becomes
binding only through a rule.

17
00:01:12,035 --> 00:01:16,922
One statement frames everything that follows,
and the chapter makes it in its first

18
00:01:16,962 --> 00:01:21,790
lines: Git can't evaluate a CODEOWNERS
file. To Git it's an ordinary tracked file.

19
00:01:21,830 --> 00:01:26,540
Every statement about how GitHub matches
patterns or requests reviews comes from

20
00:01:26,580 --> 00:01:31,229
GitHub's documentation. In the demonstration
you'll see Git's ignore matcher at

21
00:01:31,269 --> 00:01:36,097
work, and each time I'll say whether GitHub
is documented to agree with it or not.

22
00:01:36,137 --> 00:01:39,125
Where the two differ, the
difference is the lesson.

23
00:01:40,965 --> 00:01:42,543
Here's what you'll walk away with.

24
00:01:42,883 --> 00:01:44,296
After this video you can:

25
00:01:44,636 --> 00:01:48,383
say what a CODEOWNERS file does
when no rule refers to it;

26
00:01:48,723 --> 00:01:51,983
list the locations where the file
is looked up and which one counts;

27
00:01:52,323 --> 00:01:56,009
write patterns and predict the owner
of a path under "last match wins";

28
00:01:56,349 --> 00:01:59,988
name the gitignore features
that do not work in CODEOWNERS;

29
00:02:00,328 --> 00:02:02,727
explain why an owner needs write access.

30
00:02:04,567 --> 00:02:09,842
In one sentence. CODEOWNERS is a text file
in the repository that maps path patterns

31
00:02:09,882 --> 00:02:14,903
to users and teams, which GitHub uses to
request reviews automatically and, if a

32
00:02:14,943 --> 00:02:16,801
rule says so, to require them.

33
00:02:17,141 --> 00:02:21,751
Precisely. The documentation says: "Code
owners are automatically requested for

34
00:02:21,791 --> 00:02:26,107
review when someone opens a pull request
that modifies code that they own.

35
00:02:26,147 --> 00:02:30,522
Code owners are not automatically
requested to review draft pull requests."

36
00:02:30,562 --> 00:02:35,467
A pull request proposes merging a head branch
into a base branch, and a draft is one

37
00:02:35,507 --> 00:02:37,586
that still signals work in progress.

38
00:02:37,926 --> 00:02:41,395
There are three layers, and you
should be able to name them.

39
00:02:41,435 --> 00:02:46,250
The file, its content and its history belong
to Git: it's an ordinary tracked file.

40
00:02:46,290 --> 00:02:50,929
Reading the file, matching the changed
paths and requesting reviewers is done by

41
00:02:50,969 --> 00:02:55,783
GitHub. Blocking the merge until an owner
approves is also GitHub, and only through

42
00:02:55,823 --> 00:02:57,421
a ruleset or a classic rule.

43
00:02:57,761 --> 00:03:02,886
Where it lives. GitHub looks in .github/,
then in the repository root, then in

44
00:03:02,926 --> 00:03:08,052
docs/. The documentation: "If CODEOWNERS
files exist in more than one of those

45
00:03:08,092 --> 00:03:13,151
locations, GitHub will search for them
in that order and use the first one it

46
00:03:13,191 --> 00:03:18,515
finds." Each file assigns the owners for
a single branch, so main and release/1.0

47
00:03:18,555 --> 00:03:22,952
can have different owners. And the
file must be under 3 MB in size.

48
00:03:22,992 --> 00:03:28,250
A larger one, in the documentation's words,
"will not be loaded", which means no

49
00:03:28,290 --> 00:03:29,774
review requests at all.

50
00:03:30,114 --> 00:03:34,160
Syntax. Each line is a pattern
followed by one or more owners.

51
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:39,631
Comments start with a hash sign. Owners are
written as @username or @org/team-name.

52
00:03:39,671 --> 00:03:44,971
An email address that belongs to a user's
account also works "in most cases", but

53
00:03:45,011 --> 00:03:46,948
not for managed user accounts.

54
00:03:47,288 --> 00:03:50,331
Four rules of the syntax
cause most of the surprises.

55
00:03:50,671 --> 00:03:53,915
First, several owners for
one pattern go on one line.

56
00:03:53,955 --> 00:03:59,120
The documentation: "If the code owners are
not on the same line, the pattern matches

57
00:03:59,160 --> 00:04:01,351
only the last mentioned code owner."

58
00:04:01,691 --> 00:04:05,572
Second, a pattern with no owner
after it removes ownership.

59
00:04:05,612 --> 00:04:10,955
In the documented example, a line gives
/apps/ to one user and a later line names

60
00:04:10,995 --> 00:04:16,471
/apps/github with no owner. Changes under
that directory then "can be made with the

61
00:04:16,511 --> 00:04:19,329
approval of any user
who has write access".

62
00:04:19,669 --> 00:04:24,764
Third, paths are case sensitive, "because
GitHub uses a case sensitive file system",

63
00:04:24,804 --> 00:04:26,292
even when your Mac isn't.

64
00:04:26,632 --> 00:04:31,269
Fourth, an invalid line is skipped, and
the rest of the file still applies.

65
00:04:31,309 --> 00:04:35,697
So a typing error doesn't break the
file. It silently removes one line.

66
00:04:36,037 --> 00:04:40,735
Owners need write access. The textbook calls
this the rule that fails silently most

67
00:04:40,775 --> 00:04:45,246
often. The people you choose as code
owners must have write permissions for the

68
00:04:45,286 --> 00:04:49,927
repository. When the owner is a team, the
team must be visible and the team itself

69
00:04:49,967 --> 00:04:54,723
must have write permissions, "even if all
the individual members of the team already

70
00:04:54,763 --> 00:04:59,519
have write permissions directly, through
organization membership, or through another

71
00:04:59,559 --> 00:05:00,490
team membership".

72
00:05:00,830 --> 00:05:05,748
The consequence is stated plainly: "If you
specify a user or team that doesn't exist

73
00:05:05,788 --> 00:05:09,466
or has insufficient access, a
code owner will not be assigned."

74
00:05:09,506 --> 00:05:14,247
So a secret team can't be an owner. And a
team whose members can all push through

75
00:05:14,287 --> 00:05:19,087
the organization's base permission isn't
an owner until the team itself is granted

76
00:05:19,127 --> 00:05:19,855
write access.

77
00:05:20,195 --> 00:05:24,972
Why the file only requests until a rule
requires. Without a rule, a code owner is a

78
00:05:25,012 --> 00:05:29,789
suggested reviewer whom the author may
ignore. With the option "Require review from

79
00:05:29,829 --> 00:05:34,547
code owners" inside the pull request rule,
"any pull request that modifies content

80
00:05:34,587 --> 00:05:39,422
with a code owner must be approved by that
code owner before the pull request can be

81
00:05:39,462 --> 00:05:39,887
merged".

82
00:05:40,227 --> 00:05:46,113
Quick quiz. One line gives a path to two
teams, and the rule requires code owner

83
00:05:46,153 --> 00:05:50,410
review. Who has to approve? A,
both teams. B, either team.

84
00:05:50,450 --> 00:05:51,299
Your answer?

85
00:05:56,139 --> 00:06:01,532
B, either team. This is the detail that
weakens many designs: "if code has multiple

86
00:06:01,572 --> 00:06:05,983
owners, an approval from any of the
code owners will be sufficient".

87
00:06:06,023 --> 00:06:09,256
Two teams on one line means
either team, not both.

88
00:06:09,596 --> 00:06:14,350
A few more rows from the textbook's
table. A changed path with no owner: the

89
00:06:14,390 --> 00:06:19,586
code-owner requirement asks nothing for
that path. A draft: owners aren't requested

90
00:06:19,626 --> 00:06:24,696
until it's marked ready. The owner is the
author: authors can't approve their own

91
00:06:24,736 --> 00:06:29,933
pull requests, so another owner of that
path must. The textbook marks that last one

92
00:06:29,973 --> 00:06:34,664
as an inference from two documented
rules, which Lab 23.2 lets you observe.

93
00:06:34,704 --> 00:06:38,134
It's a reason to list a team,
not one person, as owner.

94
00:06:39,974 --> 00:06:44,474
Analogy. A building directory in the
lobby tells a visitor whom to call.

95
00:06:44,514 --> 00:06:49,456
It locks no door. The lock is a separate
device, a ruleset, that can be told to

96
00:06:49,496 --> 00:06:50,844
consult the directory.

97
00:06:51,184 --> 00:06:55,627
The analogy breaks in one place, and it's
the important place: this directory is

98
00:06:55,667 --> 00:06:58,597
read top to bottom, and the
last line that fits wins.

99
00:06:58,937 --> 00:07:03,502
The documentation says: "Order is important;
the last matching pattern takes the

100
00:07:03,542 --> 00:07:08,108
most precedence." So the procedure for
one path is: go through the file from the

101
00:07:08,148 --> 00:07:12,713
top, note every line whose pattern matches
the path, and keep only the last one.

102
00:07:12,753 --> 00:07:17,203
The owners on that line are the owners.
Owners from earlier matching lines are

103
00:07:17,243 --> 00:07:18,355
replaced, not added.

104
00:07:18,695 --> 00:07:22,312
The trap is a general line
placed after a specific one.

105
00:07:22,352 --> 00:07:27,498
In the sample file of the chapter, the
path router/rules/eu.yaml matches three

106
00:07:27,538 --> 00:07:32,684
lines: the star, /router/, and *.yaml.
The last of the three is *.yaml, so the

107
00:07:32,724 --> 00:07:37,404
platform and SRE teams own that path,
and the routing team isn't asked.

108
00:07:37,444 --> 00:07:42,790
The rule of thumb from the textbook: put
general patterns first and specific ones

109
00:07:42,830 --> 00:07:47,777
last, and remember that "specific" means
"later in the file", not "longer".

110
00:07:48,117 --> 00:07:52,919
How it differs from gitignore. The patterns
follow, in the documentation's words,

111
00:07:52,959 --> 00:07:57,643
"most of the same rules used in gitignore
files". The documentation warns about

112
00:07:57,683 --> 00:08:02,247
three gitignore features that don't work:
escaping a leading hash sign with a

113
00:08:02,287 --> 00:08:06,970
backslash, negating a pattern with an
exclamation mark, and character ranges in

114
00:08:07,010 --> 00:08:07,926
square brackets.

115
00:08:08,266 --> 00:08:11,835
And there's a fourth difference
that has no syntax of its own.

116
00:08:11,875 --> 00:08:16,317
Gitignore works on directories first:
when a pattern matches a directory, Git

117
00:08:16,357 --> 00:08:20,101
excludes the whole directory and
never looks at the files inside.

118
00:08:20,141 --> 00:08:23,302
CODEOWNERS assigns owners to
files, one path at a time.

119
00:08:25,142 --> 00:08:31,769
Start with two of the arrows. router/classify.py
matches lines 2 and 5, so line 5

120
00:08:31,809 --> 00:08:38,354
decides: the routing team. docs/README.md
matches lines 2 and 12: the docs team.

121
00:08:38,694 --> 00:08:43,417
Try it now, thirty seconds, on
paper. Take the first question mark,

122
00:08:43,457 --> 00:08:48,678
router/priority.py. Write down every
line that matches, and keep the last.

123
00:08:48,718 --> 00:08:49,389
I'll wait.

124
00:08:54,229 --> 00:09:00,013
Lines 2, 5 and 6 match, and line 6 is the
last. So the owners are the routing team

125
00:09:00,053 --> 00:09:04,843
and the on-call team. Both are on
line 6. The drawing only wraps it.

126
00:09:04,883 --> 00:09:10,738
The other four question marks are yours,
in the pattern exercises of section 19.16.

127
00:09:26,778 --> 00:09:31,574
Into the lab. Every command in this replay
is git check-ignore, which names the

128
00:09:31,614 --> 00:09:35,981
ignore pattern that decides for a
path. It's 🟢 SAFE: it changes nothing.

129
00:09:36,021 --> 00:09:39,042
And remember: this is Git's
matcher, not GitHub's.

130
00:09:39,382 --> 00:09:42,211
Step 1: last match wins,
where the two agree.

131
00:09:42,551 --> 00:09:47,551
Three patterns, three paths.
router/priority.py matches all three patterns.

132
00:09:47,591 --> 00:09:50,844
Which line will be named
for it? Say it out loud.

133
00:09:55,684 --> 00:10:00,667
The -v option prints the file, the line
number and the pattern that decided.

134
00:10:00,707 --> 00:10:05,028
For router/priority.py it's line
3, the last of the three matches.

135
00:10:05,068 --> 00:10:10,248
Within one file, and as long as only files
are involved, Git also lets the last

136
00:10:10,288 --> 00:10:11,834
matching pattern decide.

137
00:10:12,174 --> 00:10:13,917
Step 2: anchoring.

138
00:10:14,257 --> 00:10:18,815
Six paths. One of them won't be
listed. Which? Make your prediction.

139
00:10:23,655 --> 00:10:28,281
apps/ without a leading slash
matches an apps directory anywhere.

140
00:10:28,321 --> 00:10:34,167
That agrees with the documented CODEOWNERS
meaning, "any file in an apps directory

141
00:10:34,207 --> 00:10:39,909
anywhere in your repository". /docs/ with
a leading slash is anchored to the top

142
00:10:39,949 --> 00:10:43,714
level, so guide/docs/a.md
is missing from the output.

143
00:10:43,754 --> 00:10:47,303
And **/logs matches a logs
directory at any depth.

144
00:10:47,643 --> 00:10:49,863
Step 3: where the two part ways.

145
00:10:50,203 --> 00:10:55,132
A catch-all first, then a more specific
pattern. By "last match wins" you'd expect

146
00:10:55,172 --> 00:10:57,980
line 2 for both paths.
Predict what Git prints.

147
00:11:02,820 --> 00:11:08,493
For tests/test_classify.py Git reports line
1. Git stopped at the directory tests/,

148
00:11:08,533 --> 00:11:13,104
which the first line already matches,
and never looked at the file.

149
00:11:13,144 --> 00:11:18,541
CODEOWNERS is documented to behave
differently: in GitHub's own example, a star

150
00:11:18,581 --> 00:11:23,290
followed by *.js gives every JavaScript
file to the JavaScript owner.

151
00:11:23,630 --> 00:11:27,292
Step 4: the same mechanism
behind a documented example.

152
00:11:27,632 --> 00:11:32,865
Git ignores the nested file too. The
CODEOWNERS documentation says docs/* matches

153
00:11:32,905 --> 00:11:36,511
files directly in docs/, "but
not further nested files".

154
00:11:36,551 --> 00:11:41,849
This is the root-cause box you saw a moment
ago. If you had used this command as a

155
00:11:41,889 --> 00:11:47,318
CODEOWNERS tester, you would now believe
the docs team owns a file that falls to the

156
00:11:47,358 --> 00:11:48,230
default owner.

157
00:11:48,570 --> 00:11:52,611
Step 5: the three documented
exceptions, which all work in Git.

158
00:11:52,951 --> 00:11:57,645
In gitignore the negation on line
2 decides for config/routing.yaml.

159
00:11:57,685 --> 00:12:00,569
In CODEOWNERS it's documented not to work.

160
00:12:00,909 --> 00:12:04,664
The range matches shard-2.yaml
and not shard-7.yaml.

161
00:12:04,704 --> 00:12:08,166
In CODEOWNERS ranges are
documented not to work.

162
00:12:08,506 --> 00:12:12,503
And the escaped hash sign: a pattern
in Git, unsupported in CODEOWNERS.

163
00:12:12,543 --> 00:12:16,539
That's why a pattern that "works
locally" can do nothing in CODEOWNERS.

164
00:12:16,879 --> 00:12:18,306
Step 6: case.

165
00:12:18,646 --> 00:12:24,469
Read the two exit statuses. With
core.ignoreCase false, Docs/a.md doesn't match

166
00:12:24,509 --> 00:12:30,258
/docs/. With it true, it does. git init
switches that setting on when the file

167
00:12:30,298 --> 00:12:34,637
system ignores case, as the
default macOS file system does.

168
00:12:34,677 --> 00:12:36,566
GitHub never ignores case.

169
00:12:36,906 --> 00:12:44,230
Step 7: which file counts. Replay labs/run
ch18/codeowners-base and stop after the

170
00:12:44,270 --> 00:12:45,487
first snippet.

171
00:12:45,827 --> 00:12:51,070
The search order and the size are plain
Git questions about the base branch.

172
00:12:51,110 --> 00:12:56,632
git cat-file -e tests whether an object
exists. git cat-file -s prints its size.

173
00:12:56,672 --> 00:13:00,316
Two files exist on main. The
one in .github/ is used.

174
00:13:00,356 --> 00:13:05,460
The older docs/CODEOWNERS is dead text
that will mislead whoever finds it.

175
00:13:05,500 --> 00:13:06,156
Delete it.

176
00:13:06,496 --> 00:13:10,856
On your practice repository, following
Lab 23.2. The interface changes.

177
00:13:10,896 --> 00:13:15,876
The lab text and the linked documentation
are the reference, and no GitHub output

178
00:13:15,916 --> 00:13:20,895
was captured by the authors. In this video
only look: open the CODEOWNERS file in

179
00:13:20,935 --> 00:13:25,977
the browser after step 2 of the lab. The
documentation says errors are highlighted

180
00:13:26,017 --> 00:13:31,183
on that page. Then browse to a file under
router/ and look for the indication of who

181
00:13:31,223 --> 00:13:35,335
owns it. The enforcement part of
the lab belongs to the next video.

182
00:13:37,175 --> 00:13:38,823
Five mistakes to watch for.

183
00:13:39,163 --> 00:13:44,404
Owners are requested and ignored. Root cause:
no rule requires code owner review, so

184
00:13:44,444 --> 00:13:46,478
the file only suggests reviewers.

185
00:13:46,818 --> 00:13:51,685
The wrong team is requested. Root cause:
a general pattern stands after a specific

186
00:13:51,725 --> 00:13:55,275
one, and the last matching line
replaces the earlier owners.

187
00:13:55,615 --> 00:14:00,900
Nobody is requested for a team's line.
Root cause: the team has no explicit write

188
00:14:00,940 --> 00:14:04,647
access or is a secret team,
so no code owner is assigned.

189
00:14:04,987 --> 00:14:10,387
docs/* was meant as "everything under
docs". Root cause: that form covers files

190
00:14:10,427 --> 00:14:14,932
directly in the directory; nested
files fall to the default owner.

191
00:14:14,972 --> 00:14:16,930
The directory form is /docs/.

192
00:14:17,270 --> 00:14:22,119
Owners of one pattern were written on two
lines. Root cause: only the last line for

193
00:14:22,159 --> 00:14:23,121
a pattern counts.

194
00:14:24,961 --> 00:14:30,616
Now, out of the lab. An ML platform team
keeps service code, model configuration and

195
00:14:30,656 --> 00:14:35,905
deployment values in one repository. The
CODEOWNERS file gives /router/ to the

196
00:14:35,945 --> 00:14:41,600
routing team. Months later someone adds a
line near the end that gives *.yaml to the

197
00:14:41,640 --> 00:14:47,159
platform and SRE teams, "so that configuration
always gets a second pair of eyes".

198
00:14:47,499 --> 00:14:52,804
From that merge on, a change to
router/rules/eu.yaml requests platform and SRE and

199
00:14:52,844 --> 00:14:58,213
no longer requests the routing team, who
are the people that understand the routing

200
00:14:58,253 --> 00:15:03,362
rules. Nothing fails. No error appears.
The first sign is a routing change that

201
00:15:03,402 --> 00:15:07,989
reached production with an approval
from someone who couldn't judge it.

202
00:15:08,329 --> 00:15:12,737
The diagnosis takes one minute if you
read the file bottom-up for the path.

203
00:15:12,777 --> 00:15:17,718
The fix is to move the general line to the
top. The prevention is a review habit for

204
00:15:17,758 --> 00:15:22,462
the file itself: for every new line, ask
which existing owners lose which paths.

205
00:15:22,502 --> 00:15:24,774
That's the later line from the opening.

206
00:15:26,614 --> 00:15:32,152
Your turn. Do Exercise 23.5, "Eight
paths, one CODEOWNERS file", in

207
00:15:32,192 --> 00:15:37,313
exercises/m19-m25-github.md.
Reason from the documented rules.

208
00:15:37,353 --> 00:15:42,224
Don't use git check-ignore.
You've seen where it disagrees.

209
00:15:42,564 --> 00:15:47,004
Before you look anything up, predict for
each of the eight paths the one line that

210
00:15:47,044 --> 00:15:50,446
decides and who is requested.
Then list the faults in the file.

211
00:15:50,786 --> 00:15:55,773
The challenge is Exercise 23.7,
"Protect a monorepo", in the same file.

212
00:15:59,613 --> 00:16:03,960
Answer out loud first. A strong answer
states the rule in one sentence and

213
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:08,939
attributes it to GitHub, not to Git. It
gives a concrete file of three or four lines

214
00:16:08,979 --> 00:16:13,504
and one path, lists every line the path
matches, and names the deciding line.

215
00:16:13,544 --> 00:16:16,587
It says that earlier owners
are replaced, not added.

216
00:16:16,627 --> 00:16:21,270
It says what the symptom looks like in
practice: no error, the wrong reviewers.

217
00:16:21,310 --> 00:16:26,190
And it ends with the ordering rule that
prevents it, and how you would confirm what

218
00:16:26,230 --> 00:16:27,020
GitHub thinks.

219
00:16:28,860 --> 00:16:34,171
Let's land this. Three of the six answers
from the opening are now yours to explain:

220
00:16:34,211 --> 00:16:37,483
no rule, a later line, and
no explicit write access.

221
00:16:37,823 --> 00:16:39,483
You should now be able to say:

222
00:16:39,823 --> 00:16:44,837
CODEOWNERS is a tracked file that GitHub
reads to request reviews; only a rule makes

223
00:16:44,877 --> 00:16:46,221
those reviews required.

224
00:16:46,561 --> 00:16:52,638
GitHub looks in .github/, the root and docs/,
in that order, and uses the first file

225
00:16:52,678 --> 00:16:53,293
it finds.

226
00:16:53,633 --> 00:16:58,281
For each changed path the last matching
line decides and replaces earlier owners.

227
00:16:58,621 --> 00:17:04,218
Negation, character ranges and the escaped
hash sign do not work, docs/* covers one

228
00:17:04,258 --> 00:17:06,663
level, and paths are case sensitive.

229
00:17:07,003 --> 00:17:11,686
An owner must have write access, and a team
must be visible and hold write access as

230
00:17:11,726 --> 00:17:12,080
a team.

231
00:17:13,920 --> 00:17:21,866
Read sections 19.1 to 19.6 of Chapter 19.
Do the pattern exercises of section 19.16.

232
00:17:22,206 --> 00:17:27,151
Today you read a CODEOWNERS file the way
GitHub is documented to read it: top to

233
00:17:27,191 --> 00:17:32,324
bottom, keeping the last match. Try the
eight paths on paper before the next video.

234
00:17:32,364 --> 00:17:36,313
Next time: CODEOWNERS in force,
and why the base branch decides.

235
00:17:36,353 --> 00:17:39,553
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

236
00:17:39,593 --> 00:17:41,049
See you in the next one.
